from common import lesson, lab, cite, term, out

LESSONS = [
lesson(7,2,'the-mlp-block','The MLP block: two matrices, a GELU, and where a neuron lives',
 'Each block\'s MLP is the same small network applied at every position. Its hidden units are the "neurons" of neuron-level interpretability, and they have a definite address.',
 '''<h2 id="two-matrices">Up, nonlinearity, down</h2>
<p>The MLP reads the normalised residual vector at one position, projects it up to width 3,072 with <code>c_fc</code>, applies an elementwise nonlinearity, and projects back down to 768 with <code>c_proj</code>. Every position is processed independently with the same weights; the MLP never looks at other positions. The original Transformer's version used ReLU, FFN(x) = max(0, xW₁ + b₁)W₂ + b₂, with an inner width of 2,048 for d_model 512, the same 4× ratio GPT-2 keeps '''+cite('vaswani')+'''.</p>
<div class="math">pre = x W_in + b_in        (768 → 3072)<br>post = GELU(pre)           (elementwise)<br>out = post W_out + b_out   (3072 → 768)</div>
<p>In Hugging Face's naming, <em>W_in</em> is <code>mlp.c_fc.weight</code>, shape <code>(768, 3072)</code>, and <em>W_out</em> is <code>mlp.c_proj.weight</code>, shape <code>(3072, 768)</code>. Both are <code>Conv1D</code> layers, which despite the name compute <code>x @ weight + bias</code> with the weight stored as (in, out), the transpose of <code>nn.Linear</code>'s convention '''+cite('hf-gpt2')+'''. The capstone has to respect that orientation.</p>
<h2 id="gelu">GELU and GPT-2's approximation</h2>
<p>GELU is <em>x</em>·Φ(<em>x</em>), where Φ is the standard normal cumulative distribution function: the input scaled by the probability that a standard normal is below it. For large positive <em>x</em> it is nearly <em>x</em>; for large negative <em>x</em> it is nearly 0; in between it dips slightly below zero. Hendrycks and Gimpel give a tanh approximation, 0.5<em>x</em>(1 + tanh[√(2/π)(<em>x</em> + 0.044715<em>x</em>³)]) '''+cite('gelu')+''', and that approximation, not the exact function, is what GPT-2's config names as <code>gelu_new</code> and what the installed code computes '''+cite('hf-gpt2')+'''. At <em>x</em> = 1, the exact value is Φ(1) = 0.8413 and the approximation gives 0.8412:</p>'''+term(['python labs/anatomy.py mlp-demo'])+out('python labs/anatomy.py mlp-demo', drop=('gelu(+0)',))+'''
<p>The difference between exact and approximate GELU is in the fourth decimal place at these inputs. It still matters for the capstone: a from-scratch implementation using <code>torch.nn.functional.gelu</code> with its default exact mode will be off from Hugging Face by small amounts that compound through twelve layers. Use <code>approximate='tanh'</code> or write the formula.</p>
<h2 id="neuron">Where a neuron lives</h2>
<p>Neuron <em>n</em> of a block's MLP is three things with one index. Its <strong>input direction</strong> is column <em>n</em> of <em>W_in</em>, a 768-vector: the neuron's pre-activation is the dot product of the normalised residual with this column, plus bias <em>n</em>. Its <strong>activation</strong> is the scalar GELU(pre<sub>n</sub>) at each position, the number a "neuron fires on X" claim is about. Its <strong>output direction</strong> is row <em>n</em> of <em>W_out</em>, another 768-vector: the neuron's contribution to the stream is its activation times this row. The MLP's whole write is the sum over neurons, Σ<sub>n</sub> post<sub>n</sub> · <em>W_out</em>[<em>n</em>].</p>
<p>So "neuron 1612 in layer 0" names column 1612 of <code>h.0.mlp.c_fc.weight</code>, entry 1612 of the post-GELU tensor (TransformerLens: <code>blocks.0.mlp.hook_post[..., 1612]</code>), and row 1612 of <code>h.0.mlp.c_proj.weight</code>. The demo shows that neuron 1612 fires at 11.49 on <code>"The"</code> at position 0 and does not appear in the top three anywhere else; neuron 2845 appears in the top three at four positions. These are observations on one prompt, not characterisations.</p>
<h2 id="sparsity">Most neurons are off</h2>
<p>At each position only 7–24% of the 3,072 activations are positive. GELU is not exactly zero for negative inputs, but it is small, so most of the MLP's write at a position comes from a few hundred neurons. This is what makes neuron-level analysis tractable and also what makes it treacherous: a neuron with a large activation on your prompt may be one of thousands that fire on something, and the top-3 list changes from prompt to prompt.</p>
<h2 id="privileged">A privileged basis</h2>
<p>Unlike the residual stream, the MLP hidden layer has a privileged basis. The nonlinearity acts coordinate by coordinate, so rotating the hidden space would change the function. That is the reason neurons are candidate units of analysis at all: the 3,072 coordinates are singled out by the computation, while the 768 residual coordinates are not '''+cite('elhage')+'''. It does not follow that each neuron means one thing; a single neuron can respond to several unrelated inputs, and a feature can be spread across many neurons.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In interpretability work</span><p>A neuron explanation is a prediction about hook_post[..., n] on inputs you have not looked at. Write it as a scoring rule before looking: "on held-out text, activation above 2 occurs on tokens with property P and not otherwise." The control is a random neuron in the same layer scored by the same rule, and a version of the rule with shuffled labels. An explanation that cannot beat those has not been demonstrated; the evals course\'s module on evaluating explanations formalises this.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Reading off "top activating neurons" from one prompt and treating them as that prompt's "circuit." Top-k on one input selects for large magnitude, which correlates with how much variance a neuron has overall, not with relevance to the prediction. Compare against the neuron's activation distribution over a corpus, and against its contribution to the specific logit you care about.</p></div>''',
 'Neuron n has a large positive activation at the last position, but its output row W_out[n] is nearly orthogonal to the unembedding direction of the predicted token. What does the neuron contribute to that prediction, and what might it be doing instead?',
 'Almost nothing directly: its write is activation × W_out[n], and projecting that onto the predicted token\'s unembedding direction gives a small logit contribution. It may be writing a direction read by later blocks (an intermediate feature), or contributing to other tokens\' logits, or its write may matter only after ln_f rescales the stream. A large activation establishes that the neuron is responding to something, not that it is responsible for the output.',
 lab(['python labs/anatomy.py mlp-demo --layer 5','python labs/anatomy.py mlp-demo "The dog sat on the rug"'],
 'Run the MLP demo on layer 5 and on a different prompt at layer 0. Note which neuron indices recur. Then implement gelu_new and mlp in labs/gpt2_from_scratch.py and run the component tests.',
 'Some indices recur across prompts at layer 0 for the same tokens (" the" and " on" in particular), since layer-0 MLP activations depend mostly on the token identity and position; at layer 5 they depend on context and recur less. gelu_new(1) must equal 0.8412 to four places; the test also compares to torch\'s tanh-approximate GELU on five inputs.'),
 ['gelu','vaswani','hf-gpt2','elhage'], kw='MLP c_fc c_proj GELU gelu_new neuron hook_post d_mlp privileged basis'),

lesson(8,2,'gated-mlps','Gated MLPs: SwiGLU in Llama and Qwen3',
 'Newer models replace the two-matrix MLP with three matrices and an elementwise product. The formula is short, the pencil example is shorter, and the change matters for what "a neuron" means.',
 '''<h2 id="formula">The formula</h2>
<p>Shazeer's 2020 note proposed replacing the first matrix-and-activation of the feed-forward layer with a gated linear unit. Two parallel projections of the input are combined by an elementwise product, and one of them first passes through a nonlinearity. With the Swish nonlinearity, Swish<sub>β</sub>(<em>x</em>) = <em>x</em>·σ(β<em>x</em>), and β = 1, the variant is SwiGLU, and the whole layer is '''+cite('shazeer')+''':</p>
<div class="math">FFN<sub>SwiGLU</sub>(x, W, V, W<sub>2</sub>) = (Swish<sub>1</sub>(xW) ⊙ xV) W<sub>2</sub></div>
<p>Three matrices instead of two. The paper keeps parameter count constant by shrinking the hidden width by a factor of 2/3 relative to the ungated version, and reports that SwiGLU and GEGLU gave its best pre-training perplexities on T5-style models '''+cite('shazeer')+'''. Swish<sub>1</sub> is the same function PyTorch calls SiLU.</p>
<h2 id="pencil">A two-dimensional pencil example</h2>
<p>Take <em>x</em> = [1, 2] and 2 × 2 matrices chosen so the arithmetic is easy. Let <em>xW</em> = [0, 2] and <em>xV</em> = [3, 1]. Swish<sub>1</sub>(0) = 0·σ(0) = 0 and Swish<sub>1</sub>(2) = 2·σ(2) = 2 × 0.8808 = 1.7616, using σ(2) = 1/(1 + e<sup>−2</sup>) = 0.8808. The elementwise product with [3, 1] is [0, 1.7616]: the first coordinate is gated off entirely even though its "up" value is 3, the largest number in the example. Summing through <em>W</em><sub>2</sub> = [1, 1]ᵀ gives 1.7616.</p>'''+term(['python labs/anatomy.py swiglu-demo'])+out('python labs/anatomy.py swiglu-demo')+'''
<p>That gating-off is the point. In GPT-2's MLP a hidden unit's activation is a single nonlinear function of one dot product. In SwiGLU, each hidden unit is the product of two different dot products of the input, one squashed by Swish and one not. The gate can be zero while the up-projection is large, or the up-projection can be negative and flip the sign of a positive gate.</p>
<h2 id="which-models">Which models, verified</h2>
<p>Two sources settle which models use this. The LLaMA paper states that it replaces ReLU with SwiGLU, with a hidden dimension of 2/3 · 4d rather than 4d '''+cite('llama')+'''. The Qwen3 technical report lists SwiGLU among the components of its dense models, alongside grouped-query attention, rotary embeddings, and RMSNorm '''+cite('qwen3')+'''. The installed Hugging Face code confirms the formula for both: <code>LlamaMLP</code> and <code>Qwen3MLP</code> each compute <code>down_proj(act_fn(gate_proj(x)) * up_proj(x))</code> with <code>hidden_act = "silu"</code> and no biases '''+cite('hf-llama')+'''. The naming map is therefore:</p>
<table><tr><th>Shazeer</th><th>Hugging Face Llama / Qwen3</th><th>Shape (hidden d, inner f)</th></tr>
<tr><td>W (gate)</td><td><code>gate_proj.weight</code></td><td>(f, d) as nn.Linear; applied as x Wᵀ</td></tr>
<tr><td>V (up)</td><td><code>up_proj.weight</code></td><td>(f, d)</td></tr>
<tr><td>W₂ (down)</td><td><code>down_proj.weight</code></td><td>(d, f)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Swish₁</td><td><code>act_fn</code> = SiLU</td><td>elementwise</td></tr></table>
<p>Note the orientation difference from GPT-2: these are <code>nn.Linear</code> layers storing (out, in), while GPT-2's <code>Conv1D</code> stores (in, out). A loader that assumes one convention will silently transpose the other.</p>
<h2 id="neurons">What "a neuron" becomes</h2>
<p>In a gated MLP, hidden unit <em>n</em> still has an output direction, row <em>n</em> of <em>W</em><sub>2</sub>, and still has a scalar activation, the product Swish(<em>xW</em>)<sub>n</sub> · (<em>xV</em>)<sub>n</sub>, so neuron-level activation analysis carries over unchanged: hook the product, not either factor alone. But it now has two input directions, column <em>n</em> of <em>W</em> and column <em>n</em> of <em>V</em>, and the activation is not a monotone function of either. An explanation of a SwiGLU neuron is a claim about a bilinear-ish function of the input, and the "which direction does this neuron read?" question has two answers.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In interpretability work</span><p>Before porting a GPT-2 neuron method to a Llama-family or Qwen model, check three things against the installed model code: the activation is the gated product (hook <code>mlp.act_fn</code> output times <code>up_proj</code> output, or the input to <code>down_proj</code>), the inner width (2/3 · 4d in LLaMA, read from <code>intermediate_size</code> in the config), and the absence of biases. TransformerLens's config for such models sets <code>gated_mlp</code>; its <code>mlp.hook_post</code> is the value fed to the down-projection.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Assuming the "gate" is the branch that passes through the nonlinearity in every implementation. In the Shazeer formula and the Hugging Face code it is: <code>act_fn(gate_proj(x)) * up_proj(x)</code>. Other codebases name the branches differently. Read the forward method, not the parameter names.</p></div>''',
 'In the pencil example, keep xW = [0, 2] and change xV to [3, −1]. What is the output, and what does the sign flip tell you about reading the gate alone?',
 'Swish(xW) is unchanged, [0, 1.7616]; the product becomes [0, −1.7616]; summing through W₂ = [1, 1]ᵀ gives −1.7616. A positive gate value does not mean the unit writes in its "positive" direction; the up-projection sets the sign and magnitude, the gate sets how much passes. Hooking only the post-Swish gate would show the same value for an output of +1.7616 and −1.7616.',
 lab(['python labs/anatomy.py swiglu-demo'],
 'Recompute sigmoid(2) with a calculator (1/(1 + e^−2)) and the final 1.7616. Then read the LlamaMLP and Qwen3MLP forward methods in your installed transformers package and confirm the order of operations against the table.',
 'e^−2 = 0.1353, 1/(1.1353) = 0.8808, 2 × 0.8808 = 1.7616. In the installed code both forward methods are the single line down_proj(act_fn(gate_proj(x)) * up_proj(x)); the activation is silu because the config\'s hidden_act is "silu".'),
 ['shazeer','llama','qwen3','hf-llama'], kw='SwiGLU gated MLP GLU Swish SiLU gate_proj up_proj down_proj Llama Qwen3'),

lesson(9,2,'unembedding-and-logits','Unembedding and logits: from the stream to a distribution',
 'A final layer norm, one matrix multiply against the embedding table, and a softmax. The logit lens applies that same ending to every earlier layer.',
 '''<h2 id="ending">The last three steps</h2>
<p>After block 11 the residual stream passes through <code>ln_f</code> (lesson 04) and is multiplied by the unembedding matrix <em>W_U</em> of shape <code>(768, 50257)</code>. The result is one logit per vocabulary entry per position. GPT-2 ties this matrix to the token embedding: <code>lm_head.weight</code> is literally the same tensor as <code>transformer.wte.weight</code>, transposed in use, as the config's <code>tie_word_embeddings=True</code> and a pointer comparison both show. Vaswani et al. shared the embedding and pre-softmax matrices in the original Transformer too '''+cite('vaswani')+'''. So the logit for token <em>t</em> is the dot product of the final normalised residual with <em>t</em>'s embedding row.</p>
<div class="math">logits = ln_f(resid_post.11) · W<sub>U</sub>,  W<sub>U</sub> = wteᵀ,  shape (batch, seq, 50257)<br>p(next = t) = exp(logit<sub>t</sub>) / Σ<sub>u</sub> exp(logit<sub>u</sub>)</div>
<p>The softmax turns a row of logits into probabilities. Two of its properties are worth having in hand:</p>'''+term(['python labs/anatomy.py softmax-demo'])+out('python labs/anatomy.py softmax-demo')+'''
<p>Check the first line by hand: e² = 7.389, e¹ = 2.718, e⁰ = 1, sum 11.107, so the probabilities are 0.665, 0.245, 0.090. Adding a constant to every logit cancels in the ratio, which is why TransformerLens's <code>center_unembed</code> (subtracting the mean of <em>W_U</em>'s columns so logits are mean-zero) changes every logit but no probability. Temperature divides the logits before the softmax; lesson 12 uses it for sampling.</p>
<h2 id="logit-lens">The logit lens</h2>
<p>Nothing stops you from applying <code>ln_f</code> and <em>W_U</em> to the residual stream after block 5 instead of block 11. The result is what the model would predict if the remaining blocks did nothing. nostalgebraist's 2020 post made this the "logit lens" and observed that in GPT-2 the intermediate predictions converge toward the final output, often well before the last layer '''+cite('logit-lens')+'''. The command below does it for every block at the last position of the running prompt:</p>'''+term(['python labs/anatomy.py logit-lens'])+out('python labs/anatomy.py logit-lens')+'''
<p>Read it as a trajectory. After block 0 the stream at <em>mat</em> still mostly points at the embedding of <em>mat</em> itself, so the lens predicts <code>' mat'</code>: tied embeddings make the input token the default prediction before anything has been written. Blocks 1–7 move toward word-completions of <em>mat</em> (<em>matrices</em>, <em>mathematically</em>, <em>matting</em>); the model has not yet "decided" that the word is complete. From block 8 the predictions are plausible next words, and the final answer is a comma at 0.237. The last line confirms that the lens at block 11 is exactly the model's own output.</p>
<h2 id="what-it-shows">What the lens does and does not show</h2>
<p>The logit lens is a projection: it reads the stream in the basis the unembedding uses, and ignores everything orthogonal to it. A direction the later blocks need, but which does not itself align with any token's embedding, is invisible to the lens. So "the model knew the answer by layer 8" is a claim about one projection of the stream, and the reverse ("the model had not decided by layer 4") is weaker still, because the information may be present in a form the lens cannot read. The lens is a first application of the additive structure, not a readout of what the model "believes."</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In interpretability work</span><p>Because logits are a linear function of the final normalised stream, and the stream is a sum of writes, each write's contribution to a logit difference (say, the correct token minus a distractor) is a scalar you can compute per head and per neuron. That is direct logit attribution, and it is exact up to the layer-norm scale. The prediction test for a "this head boosts the correct answer" claim is that its attribution is positive on held-out prompts, against a random-head control.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Comparing raw logits across tools or across processing modes. TransformerLens's default compatibility mode centres the unembedding, so its logits differ from Hugging Face's by a per-position constant (108.9 on the running prompt, lesson 13), while every probability and every argmax agrees. Compare log-probabilities or logit <em>differences</em>, which are invariant to that constant.</p></div>''',
 'The lens after block 0 gives the input token itself with probability 0.92. Is that evidence that block 0 "copies the input," and what is the control?',
 'Mostly no. Tied embeddings mean the stream after embedding already has a large dot product with its own token\'s unembedding row, before any block has acted; the lens after block 0 inherits that. The control is the lens applied to the stream before block 0 (hidden_states[0] through ln_f): if it already predicts the input token strongly, block 0 added little to that. A claim about block 0 should compare its write\'s projection with the embedding\'s projection.',
 lab(['python labs/anatomy.py logit-lens "The capital of France is"'],
 'Run the lens on a prompt with a strongly expected continuation. Note at which block the final top token first appears and whether it stays.',
 'The output shows the layer at which " Paris" (if the model predicts it) first enters the top three and how its probability moves; whether it stays or is displaced is the interesting part, and it varies by prompt. Any block-level claim you form from one prompt is a hypothesis to test on a held-out set of capital-city prompts, not a finding.'),
 ['vaswani','logit-lens','hf-gpt2','tl-compat'], kw='unembedding logits tied embeddings lm_head ln_f softmax logit lens direct logit attribution'),
]
